//? Title: Find the dominant modes of variation in curves
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Extract weighted eigenfunctions and subject scores

Six synthetic response curves are measured at the same nine times. The
measurements are closer together near the ends of the time interval.
Trapezoidal weights account for the time interval represented by each
grid point when fitting the eigenfunctions and projecting the curves.

The example varies the height of a central peak and the difference
between early and late responses. Functional PCA centers the curves,
estimates the two leading eigenfunctions, and projects each subject onto
them. Each eigenvalue is the sample variance of its subject scores.

```{cpp, id=weighted-functional-pca, file="figures/functional_pca_modes.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/fda/functional_pca.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>

#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    namespace dm = datamunge;
    const std::vector<double> time{0, .5, 1.25, 2.5, 4, 5.5, 6.75, 7.5, 8};
    const std::array peak{-2., 2., -1., 1., 0., 0.};
    const std::array trend{-.5, -.5, .75, .75, -.25, -.25};
    dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> curves(peak.size(), time.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < curves.rows(); ++i)
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < curves.cols(); ++j) {
            const double bump = (time[j] - 4) / 2.25;
            curves(i, j) = 4 + (3 + peak[i]) * std::exp(-bump * bump)
                + trend[i] * (time[j] - 4) / 4;
        }
    const auto fit = dm::fda::functional_pca(time, curves, 2);

    std::println("| Component | Score variance | Integrated variance explained |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < fit.eigenvalues.size(); ++k)
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.1f}% |", k + 1, fit.eigenvalues[k],
            100 * fit.explained_fraction[k]);
    std::println("\n| Subject | Score on component 1 | Score on component 2 |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < curves.rows(); ++i) {
        const auto first = fit.scores(i, 0);
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", i + 1,
            std::abs(first) < .0005 ? 0.0 : first, fit.scores(i, 1));
    }

    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const std::array colors{dm::graphics::RGBA{24, 92, 150}, dm::graphics::RGBA{170, 74, 39}};
    const std::array labels{"Component 1", "Component 2"};
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < labels.size(); ++k) {
        std::vector<double> mode(time.size());
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < time.size(); ++j)
            mode[j] = fit.eigenfunctions(j, k);
        plot.line(time, mode, labels[k], colors[k], 2.5)
            .points(time, mode, "", colors[k], 3);
    }
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.title("Two modes of curve variation")
        .x_label("Time").y_label("Eigenfunction value")
        .y_limits(-.72, .86).size(740, 420).save_svg("build/functional_pca_modes.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Component | Score variance | Integrated variance explained |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 5.484 | 84.9% |
| 2 | 0.976 | 15.1% |

| Subject | Score on component 1 | Score on component 2 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | -3.312 | -0.835 |
| 2 | 3.312 | -0.835 |
| 3 | -1.656 | 1.253 |
| 4 | 1.656 | 1.253 |
| 5 | 0.000 | -0.418 |
| 6 | 0.000 | -0.418 |
\image(figures/functional_pca_modes.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first component changes the central peak. The second contrasts the start and end of the interval.)
\alttext(Component 1 is positive with its largest value at the center. Component 2 slopes across zero from one end of the interval to the other.)

The first mode captures most of the integrated variation. Subjects 1
and 2 have opposite first scores because the peak is respectively below
and above the mean peak. They have the same score on the second mode
because their early-to-late trend is the same.

[Ramsay and Silverman (2005), chapter 8|https://doi.org/10.1007/b98888]
